WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.692 --> 00:00:09.090
VICKI BOYKIS: Hi,
I'm Vicki Boykis.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:12.690
I'm a Machine Learning Engineer
at Automatic, the company

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.760
behind Wordpress.com.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:18.690
But today I don't want to talk
about machine learning or data.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:22.920
What I do want to talk about
is building a digital garden.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:24.600
And I want to
explain why this is

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.880
important by talking a
little bit about what

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:30.330
my daily routine looks like in
the longest year of our lives

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.740
in 2020.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.750
And I'm hoping that,
as a data scientist,

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.090
it extrapolates and
generalizes to what

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:38.160
everybody's
environment looks like,

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.410
and what we can do
about some of that.

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:44.760
So my day usually starts around
3 o'clock in the morning.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.520
That's when my
youngest son wakes up.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.420
He's very little so I usually
have to put him back to bed

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.940
and wait on him to settle down.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.790
And while I'm doing that,
I do my favorite activity,

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.950
which is doom-scrolling
through Twitter.

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:01.200
Nothing's really
changed on Twitter

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.120
since the last time I read it
maybe three or four hours ago.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:09.300
But it always makes me feel
worse coming away from it

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.220
than I do going into it.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.060
And so I'm filled with this
endless anxiety of news items

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.010
that have happened.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:19.050
So then he settles back down.

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:20.790
I go back to sleep.

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:23.460
But in the meantime, I'm
woken up around 4 o'clock

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:26.400
in the morning by a text
message from my state, which

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.280
is an alert to be
careful of COVID,

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:32.520
for the hospital
restrictions, to keep masking,

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:36.580
and to be aware that the rates
in our area are on the rise.

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:38.370
This is kind of
helpful information,

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:41.160
but not really, because
it also makes me feel

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:42.970
more anxious than relieved.

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:46.830
And it brings me more
questions than answers, really.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.900
For example, when's
the next time

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.240
I'll get to see my
elderly relatives?

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:55.170
Will school stay open for
the next couple weeks or so?

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:57.000
How are we doing as a country?

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.010
And when will it get better?

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.210
And, of course, none
of us can answer that.

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:03.900
So I'm left feeling
even more anxious.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:06.270
Of course, by the time
all of that's over,

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:07.890
I go to the numbers.

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:10.080
I look at the COVID
Tracking Project,

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.290
which is a really wonderful
project put together

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:16.770
by a group that tracks
totals across each state.

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:19.170
Although it doesn't make
me feel any better either,

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.060
even though there's
very good information.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:25.470
Because the COVID rates are
on the rise, it's exponential,

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:28.830
and it doesn't seem to
be ending any time soon.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:32.190
So by the time that's all over,
it's usually around 7 o'clock

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:35.700
in the morning, which is when
my older child, my daughter,

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:36.780
wakes up.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:40.230
And she also has a lot
of pings and needs.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:43.530
Usually hers are more like,
what do hippopotamuses eat?

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.480
And, what are the
phases of the moon?

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.520
So I help her get
ready and get dressed.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.280
And then we go
downstairs to breakfast.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:53.370
"Breakfast," I use
the term very loosely.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.920
Because as a family
with small kids,

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.860
it's just pretty much chaos.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.230
So during that time,
I get some time

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:03.630
to catch up on what's
been going on at work.

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:07.230
I usually don't have
99 Slack notifications,

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.900
but since we do work in a
distributed, remote, and

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.460
asynchronous way,
there's usually

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:15.750
someone working all around the
world, even when I'm asleep.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:19.256
So there's definitely writing
and reading to catch up on.

00:03:19.256 --> 00:03:23.190
So when that's all over, I go
to my favorite part of Twitter

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:25.500
to catch up on, which
is Data Twitter.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:27.120
And it seems like
overnight there

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.840
have been 5 million more data
analyses published, 10 million

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:34.380
more new deep learning
frameworks, and 50 million

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.210
more data controversies.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:38.170
And it's a lot to keep up with.

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:39.990
And I feel like I never can.

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:43.740
So it all adds to the
compounding anxiety

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:47.850
of the things that I have
to keep up with in 2020.

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:50.460
So after being
pinged all morning

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:52.660
and exposed to the
stressful world of 2020

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:54.930
during COVID, and the
amplification of that world

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:57.600
in my digital media,
I start hearing

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.990
not the individual noise, but
the steady stream of radio

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:02.580
KFKD.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:05.058
Radio KFKD is a concept
Anne Lamott talks

00:04:05.058 --> 00:04:07.350
about in her wonderful book
about the creative process,

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:08.730
Bird by Bird.

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:11.790
Lamott says that KFKD plays
for all creative people.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:13.560
She says there's two speakers.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.060
The left is the
noise that you hear

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:17.380
when you think you're
better than everybody else.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:19.380
And the right is the noise
when you think you're

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.000
worse than everybody else.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.480
And I count data professionals,
data professionals

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:25.620
within creative people too.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:27.450
Because we have a
lot of technical work

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:33.390
that we have to do, and a lot
of very judgmental-based work

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:38.310
that is more of an art
form than a science.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:41.790
Today we're living in radio
KFKD, but over top of that

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:43.330
is the pandemic.

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:45.780
So it's what I like
to call radio 2020.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:47.280
The right speaker's
everything we've

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:49.590
mentioned-- the noise of
other people's perfection

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:52.350
on social media, the
noise of controversy,

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:54.990
of amplified fears, of
heightened everything,

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:57.120
a platform that
is constantly on,

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:00.780
and a Black Mirror that reflects
our worst fears and beliefs.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:03.030
The left speaker is all
the news we're constantly

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:06.055
processing about living
in uncertain times.

00:05:06.055 --> 00:05:09.260
And over top of all of that
is the never-ending static

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:12.800
of the pandemic that
won't end, fears

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:16.190
for us and our loved ones,
constant restrictions,

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:18.890
and, when we need it
the most, isolation.

00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:21.440
Being isolated is hard
enough as a human.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:25.215
Being isolated and bombarded
by the radio of 2020,

00:05:25.215 --> 00:05:26.840
the noise of click-bate
doom-scrolling,

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:29.690
constantly being surrounded
by information that is largely

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:32.100
negative, is impossible.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.130
So what can we do
about all of this?

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:36.800
While we can't really
change our physical space,

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:39.350
we can't change the state
of the world right now,

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:42.350
we can't change the
people that rely on us,

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:45.620
we can't change the human
interactions that we have.

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:48.080
What we can change
is the digital world

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:49.670
that we operate in.

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:51.170
And so I want to
talk a little bit

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:54.470
about what the aspects of
that digital world are,

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:57.020
and how we can change it.

00:05:57.020 --> 00:06:01.160
Why is radio 2020 so
good at derailing us?

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:04.460
And why is it constantly
creating noise for us?

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:06.000
There's three reasons.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:09.020
First of all is that
online, it's noisy.

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:12.680
And this has been the case
since even before 2020.

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:16.160
In a conference that I attended
and spoke at, SkepTech,

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:19.790
several years ago, one of the
speakers that really stood out

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:23.510
to me was Natasha Dow
Sch��ll, who is a sociologist

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:26.780
and professor at
New York University.

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:30.290
And she wrote a called,
Addicted by Design,

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:32.990
that was talking about
all the different ways

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:36.500
that our online universes
today are a lot like casinos.

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:43.040
The summary of her research is
that much of the online world,

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:46.570
particularly social media
companies, much like casinos,

00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:50.200
are designed to draw our
attention away in small bursts.

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:52.690
As a result, to
get our attention

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:55.540
and retain you on the
platform, sites often

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:58.360
do things like send you a
combination of mobile alerts,

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:01.480
notifications, and emails.

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:03.475
Sometimes this
engagement is good,

00:07:03.475 --> 00:07:05.170
like if we're
discovering interesting

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:07.240
blog posts, or
donating to creators,

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:09.680
or watching original content.

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:12.960
But, oftentimes, it
can simply be noise.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:15.090
The online noise
interferes with our ability

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:17.310
to concentrate on
longer-term projects

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:19.950
and engage in
longer-term thinking.

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:23.250
And the problem is that there's
always new noise and always

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:24.195
new content online.

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:28.530
A second problem
with this content

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:31.470
is a lot of it is
very superlative.

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:33.840
What do I mean when
I say superlative?

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:36.420
Usually you only
get the very worst

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:38.580
or the very best
because that's what

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:42.180
actually filters through the
noise and creates engagement.

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:46.470
So, as an example, if someone
is posting their pictures

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:48.090
of vacation online--

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:50.700
although, not
recently these days.

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:54.225
But if they are, you'll usually
only see the best pictures

00:07:54.225 --> 00:07:56.010
from their hotel room.

00:07:56.010 --> 00:07:59.880
You won't actually see how they
were late for their flight,

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:04.740
or had problems at the airport,
or had transportation issues.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:06.210
You only get the highlights.

00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:08.670
And the same is true with
all the worst news stories--

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:11.040
of 2020 especially--
because those

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:13.710
are the ones that are
optimized to get us to click.

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:15.660
So we'll only see
the very, very worst.

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:19.200
And won't see what's
progressing more in the median

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:21.880
and in the long term.

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:26.380
The third problem is that
online is worse in 2020.

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:28.540
The enormous amount
of additional pressure

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:33.169
2020 has put on both our
physical and digital lives

00:08:33.169 --> 00:08:35.929
has pulled us apart as humans.

00:08:35.929 --> 00:08:38.750
Which means that we
can't meet with anybody

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:40.880
and we're left to
face the crisis,

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:43.130
in the context of the current
state of the internet,

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:43.669
on our own.

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:49.330
So we have this crisis
that's occurring

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:52.390
both in the physical world,
with everything that's

00:08:52.390 --> 00:08:54.880
been happening during
the pandemic, and then

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:57.400
the digital world, with the
crisis for our attention

00:08:57.400 --> 00:08:58.790
as well.

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:00.800
How do we fight this?

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:03.290
One of the best ways that
I have found personally,

00:09:03.290 --> 00:09:08.150
is to be able to control it
a very small bit of the time.

00:09:08.150 --> 00:09:10.730
And the best way to
do that is to build

00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:11.990
your own digital garden.

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:19.910
Anne Lamott, in Bird by Bird,
says that the best thing to do

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:22.200
is to take it piece by piece.

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:24.590
And the title of the book
comes from a book report

00:09:24.590 --> 00:09:26.900
that her brother had to write.

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:29.030
"Thirty years ago," she
writes, "my older brother,

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:31.040
who was ten years
old at the time,

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:35.090
was trying to get a
report on birds that he

00:09:35.090 --> 00:09:37.010
had three months to write.

00:09:37.010 --> 00:09:38.480
It was due the next day.

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:40.730
We were out at our
family cabin in Bolinas,

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:42.500
and he was in the
kitchen table close

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:45.710
to tears, surrounded by
binder, paper and pencils,

00:09:45.710 --> 00:09:48.440
and unopened books
by birds, immobilized

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:50.210
by the task at hand.

00:09:50.210 --> 00:09:52.143
Then my father sat
down beside him,

00:09:52.143 --> 00:09:53.810
put his arm around
my brother's shoulder

00:09:53.810 --> 00:09:55.760
and said, 'Bird by bird, buddy.

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:58.150
Just take it bird by bird.'"

00:09:58.150 --> 00:10:00.800
The idea is that we
can't change everything.

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:03.940
But even changing a little
tiny bit of our environment

00:10:03.940 --> 00:10:06.760
is better than changing nothing.

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:09.130
So what's a way that
you can fix the world,

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:10.900
and change it bird by bird?

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:13.930
The best way to do that
is by planting a garden.

00:10:13.930 --> 00:10:17.020
The Secret Garden is a book
by Frances Hodgson Burnett,

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:20.380
who lived in American
England in the 19th century.

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:22.720
The story is about
a girl named Mary

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:26.710
whose parents die in a cholera
epidemic in Colonial India.

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:28.540
It's kind of sad,
but her parents never

00:10:28.540 --> 00:10:30.130
saw her much anyway.

00:10:30.130 --> 00:10:32.570
She was cared for by
servants, and, as a result,

00:10:32.570 --> 00:10:35.800
she had grown spoiled,
selfish, and extremely lonely.

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:39.970
She's sent to England, to her
uncle's estate on the Moors.

00:10:39.970 --> 00:10:44.140
The moors are a beautiful,
lonely, and foreboding place.

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:46.540
And, just like the moors,
the house where Mary stays

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:47.470
is dark and cold.

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:50.200
And, as is often the case
in British literature,

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:51.730
full of secrets.

00:10:51.730 --> 00:10:54.130
It's revealed that
Mary's aunt, who died,

00:10:54.130 --> 00:10:57.280
had a secret garden in the
house that was then locked up.

00:10:57.280 --> 00:11:00.160
When Mary comes to the
garden, it's neglected.

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:03.730
She begins to tend to it, and
in the process of caring for it

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:06.580
and watching things grow,
she forgets to feel sorry

00:11:06.580 --> 00:11:08.290
for herself and her parents.

00:11:08.290 --> 00:11:12.170
The process of caring for
it brings her back to life.

00:11:12.170 --> 00:11:15.100
So right now we're all
a little bit like Mary

00:11:15.100 --> 00:11:17.620
at the start of a
garden that's fallow.

00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:18.760
We're lost.

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:21.260
We're not quite sure what to do.

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:23.110
So there's some
things that we can do.

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:26.740
And next I want to talk about
things that people have done.

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:28.600
And then end with
some suggestions

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:33.410
for maybe smaller things that
you can do to get started.

00:11:33.410 --> 00:11:34.730
So how do we fix this?

00:11:34.730 --> 00:11:38.080
How do we create something
that's for our public garden

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:41.070
and makes the internet
better at the same time?

00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:42.820
I'll talk about a
couple of different ways

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:44.653
that people in the
community have done this,

00:11:44.653 --> 00:11:46.330
and that I've done
this, and then

00:11:46.330 --> 00:11:49.480
offer some more lightweight
suggestions if these

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:51.340
seem overwhelming to you.

00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:54.230
The first, and best
way, in my opinion,

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:57.250
is to write something
from your perspective.

00:11:57.250 --> 00:12:00.130
David Robinson has a really
good blog post about this.

00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:03.460
He writes a bunch about
statistics, and R as well.

00:12:03.460 --> 00:12:05.380
He said that if you
write something,

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:08.110
if you tell somebody
something three times,

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:10.300
then you should write
a blog about it.

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:13.900
And he says that it's important
to write a blog for a data

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:15.700
science career portfolio.

00:12:15.700 --> 00:12:18.380
And I think it's important,
not just for that,

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:20.620
but because it allows
you to create something

00:12:20.620 --> 00:12:23.260
that you're in control
of on your own platform--

00:12:23.260 --> 00:12:26.530
that doesn't change, and
that counteracts the way

00:12:26.530 --> 00:12:28.330
that social media
works, and that you

00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:32.290
can come back to again and again
and have it always be the same.

00:12:32.290 --> 00:12:35.950
You can write about
anything from analytics,

00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:40.150
from your opinion on certain
trends, certain packages you've

00:12:40.150 --> 00:12:41.090
done.

00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:42.610
So these topics
have been written

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:44.260
about hundreds of
thousands of times

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:48.070
before data analysis
and tidyverse.

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:49.570
But the important
thing is that it's

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:50.890
coming from your perspective.

00:12:50.890 --> 00:12:53.320
Everybody comes to the story
with a completely different

00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:56.140
perspective, completely
different set of life skills,

00:12:56.140 --> 00:12:58.030
and history, and work skills.

00:12:58.030 --> 00:13:00.670
And the internet wants
to hear about them.

00:13:00.670 --> 00:13:04.450
Two really great recent examples
of this from our blogger

00:13:04.450 --> 00:13:08.430
are this recent article
about pipes, about the pipe

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:11.250
tool, and "A Spoonful of Hugo--

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:13.230
How much Hugo do
I need to know?"

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:17.740
Neither of these are very
long and complex topics.

00:13:17.740 --> 00:13:21.060
But they break down what you
need to in very specific ways.

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:24.640
And they're talking about it
from the author's experience.

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:26.700
So these are both fantastic.

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:30.760
The way that I've done this is
in a couple of different ways.

00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:34.590
One of the places I'm active
on is my technical blog.

00:13:34.590 --> 00:13:39.480
And I write about machine
learning, and data and metrics.

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:41.820
My main goal on my blog
is to write about things

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:44.910
that I don't really see anywhere
else on the blogosphere,

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:46.820
or write them from
my perspective.

00:13:46.820 --> 00:13:48.570
And the other thing
is that in the process

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:50.880
of writing more
technical posts you

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:52.950
get more insight
into what's going on,

00:13:52.950 --> 00:13:55.415
and a better
understanding as well.

00:13:55.415 --> 00:13:56.790
So some things
I've written about

00:13:56.790 --> 00:14:03.030
are getting machine learning
to production, the spark API--

00:14:03.030 --> 00:14:07.230
using R-- and in a very
serious examination,

00:14:07.230 --> 00:14:10.020
breaking down the iOS
screen time report.

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:12.120
None of these are
breakthrough topics.

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:14.850
In particular, getting
machine learning to production

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:18.120
is a topic that has been
covered over and over again.

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:19.495
But this is my
take on the topic.

00:14:19.495 --> 00:14:21.620
These are things that are
interesting and important

00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:22.200
to me.

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:26.140
And now I have them to
refer back to in my blog.

00:14:26.140 --> 00:14:28.690
Another place I've been
writing is my newsletter,

00:14:28.690 --> 00:14:32.110
"Normcore Tech," where I look
at machine learning and data,

00:14:32.110 --> 00:14:35.020
but more from a
human-centered perspective.

00:14:35.020 --> 00:14:37.330
I started, again, because
I didn't see anyone

00:14:37.330 --> 00:14:40.030
covering these topics
in quite the same way.

00:14:40.030 --> 00:14:43.335
Sure, we all talk about Google
and Facebook and social media

00:14:43.335 --> 00:14:44.710
every week, but
there were things

00:14:44.710 --> 00:14:46.917
that I wanted to
understand better.

00:14:46.917 --> 00:14:49.000
So here are some recent
things I've talked about--

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:51.040
how a lot of the
products that we use

00:14:51.040 --> 00:14:54.520
are built by consensus, by an
average of 10,000 people in

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:58.580
San Francisco, and what impact
that has for our online world.

00:14:58.580 --> 00:15:00.820
What happens when the
internet stops in a country,

00:15:00.820 --> 00:15:03.700
as it did in Belarus
in the middle of 2020?

00:15:03.700 --> 00:15:06.850
And how the internet actually
gets from country to country.

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:08.860
And how Google Drive,
which is ubiquitous

00:15:08.860 --> 00:15:10.840
in most of the
companies I've been at,

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:12.760
is actually production,
because we store

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:14.440
really sensitive data in there.

00:15:14.440 --> 00:15:17.807
And when it goes down,
it can be a problem.

00:15:17.807 --> 00:15:19.390
Another interesting
thing that happens

00:15:19.390 --> 00:15:21.850
when you write about things
that are of interest to you

00:15:21.850 --> 00:15:25.940
is you start getting people who
are also drawn to these topics.

00:15:25.940 --> 00:15:27.940
So when I first
started my newsletter,

00:15:27.940 --> 00:15:29.410
I didn't get any emails.

00:15:29.410 --> 00:15:31.390
But then, slowly and
surely, I got more people

00:15:31.390 --> 00:15:34.670
talking to me about these topics
from their perspective as well.

00:15:34.670 --> 00:15:37.460
So it's a wonderful way
for building a community,

00:15:37.460 --> 00:15:40.630
even if you didn't
initially intend it to be.

00:15:40.630 --> 00:15:42.220
The second way,
if you want to go

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:45.160
a little bit of a more technical
route, is to empower people

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:47.670
to understand data.

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:50.820
Data literacy these days
is really, really important

00:15:50.820 --> 00:15:52.980
with how confusing and
click-bait-oriented

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:55.200
the internet has become.

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:57.820
And anyone who helps
make sense of that data,

00:15:57.820 --> 00:16:00.900
and make it trustworthy,
is a superhero.

00:16:00.900 --> 00:16:03.000
A fantastic example
of this is the work

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:06.810
of Julia Silge, who has
created books, courses,

00:16:06.810 --> 00:16:10.350
and writes on her own blog
about topics like TidyTuesday,

00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:12.990
as well as showcases videos.

00:16:12.990 --> 00:16:15.390
She does a fantastic
job of explaining

00:16:15.390 --> 00:16:18.450
and making it accessible,
and has that content

00:16:18.450 --> 00:16:20.830
on her site to own.

00:16:20.830 --> 00:16:24.640
Another wonderful example is
R-Ladies Sydney course, created

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:26.740
by Lisa Williams,
who was inspired

00:16:26.740 --> 00:16:29.603
by her own frustrations
of getting started with R.

00:16:29.603 --> 00:16:31.270
The course goes through
getting started,

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:33.580
packaging, and R Markdown.

00:16:33.580 --> 00:16:35.830
And it's another
great way to work

00:16:35.830 --> 00:16:37.540
through what you're
interested in,

00:16:37.540 --> 00:16:40.390
as well as share it
with other people.

00:16:40.390 --> 00:16:43.180
Another person who has done
a wonderful job empowering

00:16:43.180 --> 00:16:45.760
people, from the Python
community this time,

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:48.910
is Ines Montani, who works in
the natural language processing

00:16:48.910 --> 00:16:52.120
space on the Python
package spaCy.

00:16:52.120 --> 00:16:53.995
And not only does she
have clear explanations

00:16:53.995 --> 00:16:57.070
of how to use the package, she
open-sourced a whole library

00:16:57.070 --> 00:17:02.530
that allows you to
create your own course.

00:17:02.530 --> 00:17:04.780
Finally, cultivating
your own garden

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:09.010
can simply be about being
human and creating connections.

00:17:09.010 --> 00:17:10.869
One of my favorite
examples about this

00:17:10.869 --> 00:17:14.410
is the illustrative work
of Allison Horst, who

00:17:14.410 --> 00:17:16.720
creates illustrations
both for her own talks

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:19.150
and for use in the R community.

00:17:19.150 --> 00:17:21.490
And they're just
beautiful, they're fun,

00:17:21.490 --> 00:17:24.190
they're interesting, and
they're creative ways

00:17:24.190 --> 00:17:27.160
to think about a topic that
hasn't really been covered

00:17:27.160 --> 00:17:29.750
in this same way before.

00:17:29.750 --> 00:17:33.200
One of the recurring themes of
"Normcore Tech," my newsletter,

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:35.900
has been that even though
we all sit in machines

00:17:35.900 --> 00:17:38.990
and write code or munch data
for a great deal of the day,

00:17:38.990 --> 00:17:41.000
we ultimately crave
human connection.

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:43.860
As we do during this
pandemic in isolation.

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:46.530
We don't have a way to
handle physical things.

00:17:46.530 --> 00:17:49.220
Especially now,
cultivating our own garden,

00:17:49.220 --> 00:17:53.960
that's not subject to the radio
frequencies of radio KFKD,

00:17:53.960 --> 00:17:57.002
is a wonderful way to do that.

00:17:57.002 --> 00:17:59.210
But what if you're overwhelmed
by the idea of getting

00:17:59.210 --> 00:18:00.560
started and don't know how?

00:18:03.190 --> 00:18:05.140
There's a lot of
really small ideas

00:18:05.140 --> 00:18:07.810
that you can draw from as
well, that don't necessarily

00:18:07.810 --> 00:18:12.140
involve creating a course,
or making videos, or creating

00:18:12.140 --> 00:18:13.270
your own blog.

00:18:13.270 --> 00:18:16.480
And so I want to cover some
of those things as well.

00:18:16.480 --> 00:18:19.480
If you're feeling intimidated
by these very large-scale,

00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:23.050
broad-ranging
projects, there's much

00:18:23.050 --> 00:18:24.490
smaller ways to get started.

00:18:24.490 --> 00:18:26.200
And, in fact, one of
the ways that I got

00:18:26.200 --> 00:18:30.430
started was by creating a blog
and drawing memes, something

00:18:30.430 --> 00:18:31.690
like 10 years ago.

00:18:31.690 --> 00:18:34.630
And I've been hooked on
content creation ever since.

00:18:34.630 --> 00:18:36.250
So there's some
much smaller ways

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:37.840
that you can approach things.

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:39.370
One of the first
things you can do

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:44.140
is just make a commitment to
write 200 words once a month.

00:18:44.140 --> 00:18:47.920
The most important thing is
that you're writing in a space

00:18:47.920 --> 00:18:50.230
that you feel
comfortable writing,

00:18:50.230 --> 00:18:51.970
and that is not an impediment.

00:18:51.970 --> 00:18:55.457
As an employee of Automatic,
I can recommend Wordpress.com.

00:18:55.457 --> 00:18:57.040
But it doesn't really
have to be that.

00:18:57.040 --> 00:19:00.550
Just any place where the
technology is not impeding you

00:19:00.550 --> 00:19:05.540
from making small progress on
a bunch of words every day.

00:19:05.540 --> 00:19:11.060
The second thing that you can
do is share a piece of code.

00:19:11.060 --> 00:19:14.120
If the idea of blogging seems
overwhelming, sharing code

00:19:14.120 --> 00:19:17.240
and talking about code can be
a stepping stone to cultivating

00:19:17.240 --> 00:19:19.220
an online place of your own.

00:19:19.220 --> 00:19:22.760
It's extremely easy to write
Markdown posts in GitHub,

00:19:22.760 --> 00:19:27.050
and an added bonus is
learning an industry

00:19:27.050 --> 00:19:28.760
tool that's very widely used.

00:19:28.760 --> 00:19:32.030
Something that I started, that
I copied from Simon Willison,

00:19:32.030 --> 00:19:35.660
is a directory called TIL, Today
I Learned-- full of random code

00:19:35.660 --> 00:19:37.130
snippets and pieces.

00:19:37.130 --> 00:19:39.020
The benefit of this
is that you come back

00:19:39.020 --> 00:19:40.730
to it again and again.

00:19:40.730 --> 00:19:43.280
I know that's true for
me, and you won't lose it,

00:19:43.280 --> 00:19:45.860
and you have it for
others to share as well.

00:19:45.860 --> 00:19:48.440
In a world of incomplete
Stack Overflow answers,

00:19:48.440 --> 00:19:50.570
think of this as your
Stack Overflow, that's

00:19:50.570 --> 00:19:53.150
filled with your specific
questions and answers

00:19:53.150 --> 00:19:55.230
for your needs.

00:19:55.230 --> 00:19:57.020
You can also make a comic.

00:19:57.020 --> 00:20:00.350
It might not seem like it,
but memes, comics, TikToks,

00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:02.720
and the like, serve as
a really important part

00:20:02.720 --> 00:20:04.220
of content creation.

00:20:04.220 --> 00:20:07.010
And I should know, my Twitter
feed is chock-full of them.

00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:08.630
Just because something
isn't serious

00:20:08.630 --> 00:20:11.750
doesn't mean it doesn't have
educational value or importance

00:20:11.750 --> 00:20:13.130
as content.

00:20:13.130 --> 00:20:15.590
I'm thinking specifically
of the wonderful zines

00:20:15.590 --> 00:20:17.360
that Julia Evans puts
out, that include

00:20:17.360 --> 00:20:21.110
things like learning Linux and
Git, but in a fun way.

00:20:21.110 --> 00:20:23.070
You don't have to create
even an entire zine.

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:25.880
Even a fun meme about R,
statistics, or drawing,

00:20:25.880 --> 00:20:28.310
like Allison Horst,
are all great ways

00:20:28.310 --> 00:20:30.290
to share what you
know and feel like you

00:20:30.290 --> 00:20:34.050
have some sort of control
over the creative process.

00:20:34.050 --> 00:20:35.750
You can also do
something like make

00:20:35.750 --> 00:20:37.670
a bot, which is what I did.

00:20:37.670 --> 00:20:39.530
If you like and
have the opportunity

00:20:39.530 --> 00:20:42.560
to write code in your
spare time, building a bot,

00:20:42.560 --> 00:20:45.320
based off of an API,
a fun way to learn

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:49.160
about how the web works and
claim your little piece of it.

00:20:49.160 --> 00:20:51.620
A few years ago, I built
something called Soviet Art

00:20:51.620 --> 00:20:53.720
Bot, which, as it
says on the tin,

00:20:53.720 --> 00:20:56.660
scrapes WikiArt for
socialist realist images

00:20:56.660 --> 00:20:58.590
and tweets them out once a day.

00:20:58.590 --> 00:21:00.870
I learned a lot
during this process.

00:21:00.870 --> 00:21:02.390
But what I love
about it is that I

00:21:02.390 --> 00:21:05.210
get to control my online
environment through code.

00:21:05.210 --> 00:21:08.900
I'm creating and changing what
people see on their timelines.

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:12.270
Art is something I always
appreciate on my Twitter feed.

00:21:12.270 --> 00:21:14.660
And as an additional
bonus, it gave me

00:21:14.660 --> 00:21:18.110
the time to learn about Twitter
APIs and Cloud computing

00:21:18.110 --> 00:21:20.270
paradigms.

00:21:20.270 --> 00:21:23.540
The three things that
I just talked about

00:21:23.540 --> 00:21:25.790
are very tangible
ways, but there's also

00:21:25.790 --> 00:21:28.730
very intangible ways to do so.

00:21:28.730 --> 00:21:31.670
The first one is sending
an email appreciation--

00:21:31.670 --> 00:21:34.700
either an email, or
a note on GitHub,

00:21:34.700 --> 00:21:36.920
or a comment on someone's blog.

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:39.660
It can be to the author of
your favorite R package,

00:21:39.660 --> 00:21:41.960
someone you're working
with, or just someone you've

00:21:41.960 --> 00:21:43.730
seen do cool stuff online.

00:21:43.730 --> 00:21:45.650
People love getting
appreciation.

00:21:45.650 --> 00:21:48.740
And in an increasingly
negative and polarized world,

00:21:48.740 --> 00:21:51.710
that kind of feedback is
really hard to come by.

00:21:51.710 --> 00:21:53.300
You might even get
into a conversation

00:21:53.300 --> 00:21:55.980
with the author about something
and create something together,

00:21:55.980 --> 00:21:58.130
which has happened to me before.

00:21:58.130 --> 00:21:59.690
What I love about
this, especially

00:21:59.690 --> 00:22:02.120
with the email aspect,
is that it's private,

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:05.210
but it's still a way to send out
really great, positive energy

00:22:05.210 --> 00:22:06.810
out in the world.

00:22:06.810 --> 00:22:10.048
And finally, start
your own community.

00:22:10.048 --> 00:22:11.840
One of the reasons that
we feel overwhelmed

00:22:11.840 --> 00:22:14.900
is that big communities
are intimidating.

00:22:14.900 --> 00:22:16.940
I've often talked about
this theory I have

00:22:16.940 --> 00:22:19.040
called good things don't scale.

00:22:19.040 --> 00:22:20.900
Where one of the reasons
we get stressed out

00:22:20.900 --> 00:22:23.630
about social media,
is that humans are not

00:22:23.630 --> 00:22:25.940
meant to carry out
as many relationships

00:22:25.940 --> 00:22:27.650
as are in Dunbar's number--

00:22:27.650 --> 00:22:29.060
150 people.

00:22:29.060 --> 00:22:31.310
We simply can't hold
it in our heads.

00:22:31.310 --> 00:22:34.970
And so we lose context with
all the conversations we have.

00:22:34.970 --> 00:22:38.750
The best way to fight that noise
is to create a small community.

00:22:38.750 --> 00:22:40.940
That can, again,
be in any medium.

00:22:40.940 --> 00:22:43.460
As long as it's a medium
that you and the other people

00:22:43.460 --> 00:22:45.180
in the community keep up with.

00:22:45.180 --> 00:22:47.360
For example, if you know
you hate text messages,

00:22:47.360 --> 00:22:49.160
use Signal or WhatsApp.

00:22:49.160 --> 00:22:53.720
The ideal community for
this is under 10, 15 people.

00:22:53.720 --> 00:22:57.020
And these people should
be fairly active.

00:22:57.020 --> 00:22:59.720
A community of like-minded
people who support each other

00:22:59.720 --> 00:23:01.640
is the key to
fighting the bigness

00:23:01.640 --> 00:23:03.860
and the noise of the internet.

00:23:03.860 --> 00:23:05.810
Because the process
for caring for a garden

00:23:05.810 --> 00:23:10.100
is an important one, and
it's a long standing process,

00:23:10.100 --> 00:23:13.550
and it's made up of a
very large, broad range

00:23:13.550 --> 00:23:15.140
of multiple things.

00:23:15.140 --> 00:23:17.360
Every time you create
something positive

00:23:17.360 --> 00:23:19.790
you're doing a small
amount of work outshining

00:23:19.790 --> 00:23:22.940
all of the negative work in the
world in the internet today.

00:23:22.940 --> 00:23:25.460
You are taking away one
piece of noise, one ping,

00:23:25.460 --> 00:23:27.670
from your life.

00:23:27.670 --> 00:23:29.420
There are many reasons
to do it, including

00:23:29.420 --> 00:23:31.710
the ones I talked about at
the beginning of the talk.

00:23:31.710 --> 00:23:33.560
But mostly, for me
personally, it's

00:23:33.560 --> 00:23:36.950
a wonderful way to manage
my own 2020 anxiety

00:23:36.950 --> 00:23:38.883
and change the
world bird by bird.

00:23:38.883 --> 00:23:40.550
Because when you're
tending to a garden,

00:23:40.550 --> 00:23:42.620
a thistle cannot
grow in that place,

00:23:42.620 --> 00:23:44.842
as Frances Hodgson
Burnett writes.

00:23:44.842 --> 00:23:46.550
And maybe that's just
as much of a reason

00:23:46.550 --> 00:23:48.890
to create a garden as any other.

00:23:48.890 --> 00:23:52.550
It's my hope that
radio 2021 is going

00:23:52.550 --> 00:23:55.610
to be tuned to a much, much
better frequency than radio

00:23:55.610 --> 00:23:58.240
2020.

00:23:58.240 --> 00:24:00.190
Thank you so much
for your time and I

00:24:00.190 --> 00:24:03.540
hope to see your
digital gardens soon.

